Subject: [PATCH] EE thread (FPS): calls left unfinished continued in place, cheaper checkpoints, scratchpad stores, DMA chains

The game thread (EE) is the frame-rate limit in levels (TODO 62/94). Each change runs the same guest
code in the same order with the same EE clock (a scripted cold boot into Amazon with VIF1 on the EE
thread: the game's state identical on every frame before and after, also with the emulated IOP
sound server).

- A call left unfinished with nothing due (PS2Runtime::dispatchGuestBranch): the callee went on
  somewhere else - a switch's jump table into a case body the function analysis made a function of
  its own, or a branch into an epilogue shared with the function after it. Amazon: ~650,000 times
  a second, mostly in two game functions (0x258690, 0x1FFC70). The whole host stack returned to the
  dispatcher, which entered that address and then every caller again one by one. Now the code is
  entered in place, with the dispatcher's checkpoints (8 cycles) at the same points: before each
  address it would have entered and before each caller goes on (resumedByDispatcher). A checkpoint
  due on the way takes the old way from there. PS2X_DISPATCH_CONTINUE=0: as before.
- The checkpoint (EeScheduler::checkpointDue, every call and loop back edge) inline in
  eeCheckpointDue and dispatchGuestBranch; the deadline and time-slice tests one compare
  (m_fastEndCycle); the cycles for the EE timers and the IOP held until one of them could act on
  them (accountCyclesInline / flushHeldCycles) - their own fast paths only added them up, so they
  get the same cycles at the same checkpoint. A timer register, a call into the IOP or a save state
  hands them on first (ps2EeFlushHeldCycles); what moves their next point forward asks for the
  limit again (ps2EeInvalidateHeldCycles).
- Stores to the scratchpad straight into it (Store8..Store128): the game keeps much of its working
  state there and each went through PS2Memory::write*'s address translation. The DMA completions'
  drain tests its flag before the call.
- DMA chains: a tag's VIF codes and the payload right behind it copied as one stretch.
- VIF1: the scan tests UNPACK first; the queue's clock is read only for PS2X_MTVU_STATS.
- The dispatcher's per-thread state (history, levels) in one thread-local.
- PS2X_EE_STATS=1 (debugging aid): every 2 s how often the dispatcher enters guest code again and
  why (deadlines, slices, invocations, timer interrupts, the addresses, the calls left unfinished).

On a GTX 1080 / Ryzen 5 5600 test PC, Amazon from its first gate, the user's settings at 1080 lines,
120 held, game ticks 8600-13000, with the native capture's own changes (port commit):
58.3-58.9 -> 72.0-72.5 fps (17.0 -> 13.8 ms a frame). The other PC (RX 5700 XT): 80.3 -> 93.1 fps.
